all right let's get into audience  questions for the week of course you can  send them in however you want email  voicemail Twitter reddit whatever you  want today's first question is via  voicemail the voicemail number is two  one nine two david p hey david i wanted  to ask you about the Laffer curve I hear  a lot of people on the Left often when  putting the idea of the Laffer curve  because it seems to be associated with  Reaganomics and the extent to which that  it is you know I can understand this  kind of criticism but I just want to  hear from you in theory why the idea is  not sound because my understanding of it  is that at a zero percent tax rate the  government will take in zero dollars in  revenue obviously at a hundred percent  tax rate the government will take in  zero percent revenue because they will  completely destroy any kind of  productivity so all to my mind the  Laffer curve says you correct me if I'm  wrong is that there's just a number  somewhere in between 0 and 100 that's  optimal obviously people on the right  will tend to say that that's a lower  number and use this idea as a  justification to reduce tax rates but I  don't understand why I'm theory and  liberals could very easily apply the lab  refer to and say the ideal tax rate is  actually higher so I don't understand  why the lapper curve is kind of always  associated with the political right in  this country when it seems to me that  it's sort of a neutral thing that either  side could use and it seems like sort of  a common-sense idea so I'd like to hear  your take on this thanks David  yeah so listen the the right uses the  Laffer curve because they like to come  up with just really simple ways of doing  politics like they've been really  successful by saying hey taxes or taxes  are bad because it's your money you've  earned it and you should get to keep it  so taxes should be really low or  abortion is bad because you're killing a  kid that's it and they really like these  simple ideas so the Laffer curve has  been used almost exclusively by the  right to justify cutting taxes you're  right it could hypothetically be used by  the left but it's not because the left  tends to take a more nuanced view on  this and I'll explain what I mean  so yes the Laffer curve is the idea that  given some tax rate  between 0% and a hundred percent there  is some optimal optimal number but more  importantly and this is where the right  tends to use it they often like to argue  that a reduction in the tax rate can  lead to an increase in government  revenue that basically the idea would be  if you sometimes lower the tax rate  typically that's what the right wants to  do you will generate more total revenue  by more people working because the tax  rate on their income is lower and  because there will be more money  floating around for economic growth here  are the problems with the Laffer curve  number one it's usually used to justify  a reduction in taxes when actually an  increase in taxes would be better for  the economy so the Laffer curve is  constantly misused by the right but the  most important thing is that the tax  rate that the Laffer curve is referring  to is like an overall rate or an average  rate of some kind and as we know we have  a progressive tax system so the impact  that a change in the tax rate is going  to have on government revenue is going  to be very different based on who is  getting the tax increase or the tax  decrease you could have a decrease or an  increase to the average tax rate in a  way that would be stimulative to the  economy or not if you give all if you  decrease taxes by reducing the tax rate  only on the richest Americans your  economic benefit is going to be far  smaller than if you lower the average  tax rate by giving money back to  middle-class Americans because  middle-class Americans need the extra  money for their daily lives whereas the  very rich are just going to save that  money which is not very economically  stimulative so that's why the right Pat  likes to oversimplify a lot of issues  and they say hey the Laffer curve tells  us you lower taxes revenue goes up the  average tax rate that the Laffer curve  deals with does not actually tell us  anything about what would happen to the  economy which tax bracket are you  talking about yeah this is what  Republicans are trying to argue now  right with their tax plan yeah you know  even though revenues will go  we'll make up for it because there'll be  so much more economic productivity  indeed but then you have independent  agencies analyzing it and they predict  maybe a tenth of the economic output  that Republicans are predicting that's  exactly right and you could have this is  not what people I think don't understand  is we're not making an argument for or  against raising or lowering taxes on any  particular tax bracket in this segment  what I'm telling you is that the Laffer  curve is effectively useless if we're  talking about the average tax rate  because we have a progressive tax system  in the US with brackets and it's almost  always misused by the right just to  justify lowering taxes all the time  which is exactly what they want to do so  yes Laffer curve it's interesting it's  not particularly useful to us to in  thinking about what it is we need to do  with tax ball yeah I love it zero to 100  percent somewhere in the middle of there  but it tells us nothing about where the  top of that parabola is like where does  it max out and not to mention what the  brackets should be and at what income  limits you go from from being in one  bracket to the other which are really  the most important thing so you it's  interesting to know about the Laffer  curve I don't know how much it's gonna  teach you about what taxes should be

